= Additional thoughts/notes =

In pulling this together, I found a few things after looking up language after language:

* There is a site dedicated to counting systems that I have pulled from heavily, https://www.languagesandnumbers.com/, although it's often surprising which languages they include and which they don't
* Someone else has been compiling a small database of number systems of the world: http://www.sf.airnet.ne.jp/~ts/language/number.html
*: They haven't gotten very far, but it's a neat site. But they have a link to a few sites with a much more complete set, in particular:
*:* Numbers from 1 to 10 in over 5000 languages: http://www.zompist.com/numbers.shtml
*:* A site that hasn't worked since 2002: https://web.archive.org/web/20020523094502/http://euslchan.tripod.com/index.html
*:*: Most of the numbers are embedded in images, and often using phonetic characters that aren't really part of the Latin set, so not useful for this exercise, but a good reference
* Wiktionary has been building some category pages for numbers in various languages (see [[:wikt:Category:Maori cardinal numbers]] for an example) and in a few cases they have an appendix page with a list or table (see [[:wikt:Appendix:Maori numerals]]), but there is a very long way to go there
* Omniglot is always useful for looking up languages, but sometimes their orthography in Latin letters is not the one I would use. But they do have dedicated number pages for many languages (e.g., https://omniglot.com/language/numbers/maori.htm)
* There is an [https://abvd.shh.mpg.de/austronesian/ Austronesian Basic Vocabulary Database (ABVD)] that is very helpful for a lot of the harder to look up Austronesian languages (like [[#Ma'anyan]])



